CORRECTION to this commit's message.

This commit contains ONE file: tools/hooks/post-commit. The message describes it
as also carrying the three W21b worklogs. It does not.

Those three were committed by a concurrent agent in be444f9, seconds before this
commit was written. `git add` on already-committed, unmodified files stages
nothing, so the commit correctly reduced to the hook alone -- but the message had
already been written and was not corrected before committing.

Everything the message says ABOUT the reviews and the hook is accurate and was
verified. Only the claim about what this commit delivers is wrong.

The near-miss worth recording: this commit's `git read-tree HEAD` was taken
BEFORE be444f9 landed. Had that agent committed a MODIFIED version of a file
this one also touched, this commit would have reverted it -- the stale-read-tree
failure the handover documents, reproduced here despite following the documented
ordering. The post-commit hook fixes the frozen-index symptom; it does NOT make
concurrent commits safe. The actual rule is that one process commits at a time,
and that rule was broken while two agent workflows were running.
